{#- Calendar subscribe box on a water page (DECISIONS 0062; on a schedule
    state's water page too, DECISIONS 0064).
    Needs: water, canonical_url, app_store_url, root.
    Optional, each defaulting to California's words so a California page
    renders byte for byte as before: calendar_agency ("CDFW"), calendar_each
    ("each week"; Tennessee: "each day or week"), calendar_what ("new weeks";
    Tennessee: "new dates"), calendar_new ("in a new week"; Tennessee: "on a
    new date") and calendar_placement ("water"; a state page: "state_water",
    as its gear block reports).

    It sits inside the status block, right after the badge, so on a phone it
    is on the first screen, next to the facts it subscribes to. Before 0062
    it was one inline sentence about 1.2 to 1.6 phone screens down, and its
    only subscribe link was webcal://, which Android does not open without
    an extra app.

    Three ways in, each labeled by the calendar it works with. The visible
    copy names no phone platform other than Apple's: until the Android app is
    on Google Play, the privacy page is the only page that names Android
    (test_site_app_store.py).
    - webcal:// for iPhone, iPad and Mac (Apple Calendar subscribes and keeps
      polling; an https .ics link would only import today's copy);
    - Google Calendar's add-by-URL link, the way in on Android phones, which
      opens calendar.google.com and adds the same feed as a subscription;
    - the plain https .ics file as a one-time copy, plus its address for any
      other calendar app.
    Each link carries calendar_subscribe_click with its own data-ga-type, so
    the report can tell the three apart. The event is a tap, not a confirmed
    subscription: nothing here can see whether a calendar added the feed. -#}
{% set feed_https = canonical_url ~ "feed.ics" %}
{% set webcal_url = "webcal://" ~ canonical_url.split("://", 1)[1] ~ "feed.ics" %}
{% set google_url = "https://calendar.google.com/calendar/r?cid=" ~ (webcal_url|urlencode|replace("/", "%2F")) %}
{% set cal_agency = calendar_agency|default("CDFW") %}
{% set cal_each = calendar_each|default("each week") %}
{% set cal_what = calendar_what|default("new weeks") %}
{% set cal_new = calendar_new|default("in a new week") %}
{% set cal_placement = calendar_placement|default("water") %}
<div class="calendar-subscribe" role="group" aria-labelledby="calendar-heading">
  <h3 id="calendar-heading">Get {{ water.name }}'s {{ cal_what }} in your calendar</h3>
  <p>
    Subscribe once: {{ cal_each }} {{ cal_agency }} schedules {{ water.name }} shows up in your
    calendar, which checks for changes on its own. Free; Trout Truck needs no
    account or email address.
  </p>
  <ul class="calendar-subscribe__links">
    <li><a class="calendar-subscribe__button" href="{{ webcal_url }}" data-ga-event="calendar_subscribe_click" data-ga-placement="{{ cal_placement }}" data-ga-type="webcal">Apple Calendar (iPhone, iPad, Mac): subscribe</a></li>
    <li><a class="calendar-subscribe__button" href="{{ google_url }}" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-ga-event="calendar_subscribe_click" data-ga-placement="{{ cal_placement }}" data-ga-type="google">Google Calendar (any phone or computer): add it</a></li>
  </ul>
  {#- The third way in, web push (DECISIONS 0069), inside this same box so it
      never competes with the calendar buttons for the first screen. Only
      when the build has a push config and the page names its water. -#}
  {% if push and push_water_id %}
  {% include "_push_subscribe.html.jinja" %}
  {% endif %}
  <p class="muted">
    Google Calendar opens in your browser; once added, the calendar shows in
    Google Calendar on your phone too, and Google can take up to a day to show
    a change. Other
    calendar apps: subscribe to
    <span class="calendar-subscribe__url">{{ feed_https }}</span>, or
    <a href="feed.ics" data-ga-event="calendar_subscribe_click" data-ga-placement="{{ cal_placement }}" data-ga-type="ics">download a one-time copy (.ics)</a>, which does not update.
    {% if app_store_url %}
    For a notification when {{ water.name }} appears {{ cal_new }}, <a href="{{ app_store_url }}" data-app-store-placement="water" data-app-store-kind="link">get the iOS app</a>.
    {% else %}
    An iOS app with a notification when {{ water.name }} appears {{ cal_new }} is on its way (<a href="{{ root }}about/#app">how it will work</a>).
    {% endif %}
  </p>
</div>
